// The three shapes `str_buf_append` has to handle, and the buffer it replaces.
//
// Appending copies out of the buffer it is handed and hands back a different one.
// The parser already treats that as a transfer rather than a replacement: it does
// not set `frees_old` for an assignment whose right side is this call. Nothing
// releases the old buffer, so it leaks -- ASan reported 4285 bytes across 12
// allocations for a loop that appends one byte two thousand times, twelve rather
// than two thousand because the buffer grows geometrically.
//
// The release cannot simply be added on the runtime side. This program works
// today and stops working if the call frees what it replaced:
//
//     var b = str_buf_new(0);
//     Console.WriteLog(len(str_buf_append(b, "grown")));
//
// The call returns a different buffer and `b` still names the old one, which the
// scope teardown then releases: two releases of one pointer. Modelling the
// transfer properly means the compiler marking the argument consumed, which is a
// separate piece of work. So the leak stands and this case exists to pin the
// behaviour that is correct today, and to notice if either half moves.
//
// Every call below stores the result back over the variable it came from, which
// is what the standard library does and the only shape that is safe. The one that
// is not -- a call result passed straight to something else -- is in the comment
// above rather than here, because a golden test cannot observe a double free
// without dying on it.

int main() {
    // A runtime-allocated buffer, reallocated on the first append and not on the
    // ones after it.
    var b = str_buf_new(8);
    b = str_buf_append(b, "grown past the start");
    b = str_buf_append(b, " and again");
    Console.WriteLog(b);

    // A literal. It has no spare capacity by definition, so every append to it
    // reallocates, and the runtime must not free the literal it copied out of.
    var l = str_buf_append("lit", "eral");
    l = str_buf_append(l, " and more");
    Console.WriteLog(l);

    // A buffer with room, which takes the in-place path and releases nothing.
    var roomy = str_buf_new(64);
    roomy = str_buf_append(roomy, "small");
    Console.WriteLog(roomy);
    Console.WriteLog(len(roomy));

    // And through the builder, which is what the standard library actually calls.
    var sb = new StringBuilder();
    sb.append("a");
    sb.appendInt(42);
    sb.append("b");
    Console.WriteLog(sb.toString());

    return 0;
}
